{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And YHWH opened the mouth of the she-ass.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the speech of the she-ass was a great miracle, outside the order of nature, and this was for the honor of Israel. For the Holy One, blessed be He, did a wondrous thing and wished to alter the orders of creation through the speech of an animal, to say that even the animal would recognize and know that this mission was not fit to be carried out. And this is like a person who says to the mountains that they should cover him and to the hills that they should fall upon him — and no need to mention the human species, which is intelligent, for even the animal, which has no intellect, would grasp that it is not fit to agree to cursing a people, for they are blessed." "<b>And if</b> you grasp in the hidden [sense] the matter of this pericope, you will find that the speech of the she-ass is like the speech of the serpent — and this one and that one, not of their own knowledge, for there is in them no rational soul. And the reason for this follows from 'and YHWH opened the mouth of the she-ass' and 'and YHWH uncovered the eyes of Balaam and he saw the angel of YHWH': for as soon as she spoke and died, the angel was revealed to him and spoke with him. And therefore Scripture mentions in the pericope three times 'and the she-ass saw the angel of YHWH' — for the swerving from the road, and the crushing of the foot, and the pressing against the wall, which are three signs, each and every one of them was from the side of the angel — and no need to mention the fourth sign, which is the speech." "<b>And consider</b> the speech of the angel who said to him, 'Wherefore have you struck your she-ass these three times?' — it is set on a par with the speech of the she-ass, who said to him, 'What have I done to you, that you have struck me these three times?' And you will grasp here [the matter of] 'three men standing' who were actual men and were actual angels, and this you will understand from the words of the angel, who mentioned to him 'I went out,' when he could have said 'Here I am, an adversary,' but instead said 'I went out' — that is, after the speech of the she-ass I went out, I, to speak to you and to be an adversary to you, and this is clear."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:13.527477921Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}